          "content": "Example text\n",
          "id": "b_mr8biycyf1m1ff",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "content": "When you are given a story dossier, create a fleshed-out list of all important setting and worldbuilding elements needed specifically for this book. Make sure each worldbuilding element is labelled as one of the following categories:\n> \n> - High-level Worldbuilding\n> - Setting(s)/Locations\n> - Objects/Artifacts\n> - Magic Systems/Technology\n> - Groups/Races\n> - Gods/Deities\n> - Geography/Nature\n> - Population/Politics\n> - Culture\n> - History/Lore\n> - Religion/Beliefs\n> - Languages \n> - Do not use a category if it doesn't apply to the book, or if there aren't elements in the dossier that apply to that category. Make sure to fully flesh out all of the worldbuilding details that a writer might need to write this story. Each worldbuilding element should feel fully fleshed out, not just set dressing. Each worldbuilding element should have at least 3-4 sentences describing it. Make the details specific. Only include the asked-for worldbuilding details. Do not add any preamble, commentary, or anything other than what has been asked.",
          "id": "b_mr8bj54i6yh9l5",
          "type": "callout"
        }
      ],
